const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Patient Education Content Service","serviceType":"Patient-facing health information written to a measured reading level and clinically reviewed","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/patient-education-content/","description":"Patient leaflets, discharge instructions and participant information written to a stated reading grade and measured with SMOG and Flesch–Kincaid.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What reading level should patient education materials be written at?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Grade 5 to 6 if you want a single answer, and that answer comes from AHRQ: its Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit says that to ensure wide understanding it is best for materials to be written at the 5th or 6th grade level, noting that the average adult reads at 8th to 9th grade and about a fifth of adults read at 5th grade or below. That is the only current federal source naming a number. The CDC does not currently state a grade-level target — it gives technique instead, such as averaging twenty words per sentence and limiting each sentence to one idea — and the NIH explicitly declines to set one, saying readability scores are not the be-all and end-all and that what matters is whether the document communicates clearly. So “the CDC recommends 8th grade” and “the NIH recommends 6th grade” are attributions neither body made, and both circulate widely. Where an ethics committee or a regulator specifies a different target, theirs governs."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between SMOG and Flesch–Kincaid?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Flesch–Kincaid grade uses average sentence length and average syllables per word. SMOG — the Simple Measure of Gobbledygook, from McLaughlin in 1969 — estimates the years of education needed to understand a passage, based on how many words of three or more syllables it contains. SMOG runs roughly one to two grades higher than Flesch–Kincaid on the same text, which is the conservative direction and the main reason health literacy work tends to prefer it for safety-critical material. The CDC records that SMOG predicts grade-level difficulty within 1.5 grades in 68% of passages. Two practical notes: SMOG was designed for samples of at least thirty sentences, so a figure from a single paragraph extrapolates and should be treated as a signal rather than a measurement; and no formula detects whether the meaning is right, only how hard the words and sentences are. A leaflet that has softened a threshold scores better, not worse."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is PEMAT, and what counts as a good score?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Patient Education Materials Assessment Tool, published by AHRQ. It scores two separate domains — understandability and actionability — and it is not a readability instrument. PEMAT-P covers printable material with 24 items, 17 on understandability and 7 on actionability; PEMAT-A/V covers audiovisual material with 17 items. Each item is scored agree or disagree, some are not applicable, and the result is reported as a percentage per domain. On what counts as good: AHRQ does not set a threshold. The user’s guide says only that the higher the score, the more understandable or actionable the material. The 70% cut-off quoted almost universally across this industry is a convention that came out of the secondary literature, not an AHRQ standard, and presenting it as one is the kind of small false certainty that this field is full of. We report both percentages and say what drove them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does simplifying the language change the clinical meaning?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It can, and that is the real risk in this work. In the rewrite shown above, three clinical changes were introduced by the simplification itself: a temperature threshold of 38°C became “if you feel feverish”, an instruction not to drive for 48 hours became advice to avoid driving until you feel ready, and a red flag about bleeding soaking through a dressing became reassurance that some bleeding is normal. Every one of those is more readable. Every one is clinically different. And none was visible to the competent person who wrote them. A readability formula cannot detect any of it, because the softened version scores better. The only thing that catches it is a clinician reading the plain version against the source, which is why that step is included on every tier and why we will not sell a rewrite without it. Three categories need particular care: numbers, absolutes and red flags."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"We have fifty leaflets already. Where do we start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With an audit rather than with new material, which is almost always the honest recommendation and rarely what the enquiry asks for. Grade all fifty, which is quick, and you will typically find three things: an average around grade 11 or 12, no version numbers or review dates anywhere, and three or four different phone numbers across the set because the service moved and only some leaflets were updated. That last one is the most urgent: a leaflet sending a patient to a number that closes at five, read at seven in the evening, converts somebody who would have attended into somebody who waits. From the audit you get a prioritised list — usually the discharge instructions and the red-flag material first, because those carry the consequence — and a much smaller rewrite budget than commissioning fifty new pieces. It also usually reveals that eight of the fifty duplicate each other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle translation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As a rewrite in the target language, not as a substitution. A leaflet translated word for word keeps the source language’s sentence structure and its grade, and frequently lands harder in the target language than the original did in English — so the translated version is measured in its own right against its own target, which is the step almost nobody does. Beyond the formulas, cultural adaptation covers what a reader is assumed to already know, who they are likely to ask for advice, and what the health system looks like where they live: an instruction to “contact your GP” means nothing in a country with no registered-list primary care. Practical note on packages: the tiers cap the languages included, but there is no limit beyond your budget — further languages are quoted per language. Rather than advertise “more than 20 languages” next to packages capped at one, two and three, this page states the cap in each package and prices further languages separately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do we know the material actually works?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not from a readability score, which is a proxy. The CDC’s own guidance is that formulas should be used only alongside other means of assessing effectiveness, and that pretesting with real members of the audience is the most reliable evaluation there is. Practically, that means showing the draft to eight or ten people from the actual population and asking them to tell you in their own words what they would do next — not whether they liked it, and not whether they understood it, because almost everybody says yes to both. The questions that work are behavioural: what would you do if this happened; when would you ring; what is normal. If they cannot answer, the leaflet has not worked however it scores. That testing is yours to run and it takes an afternoon. Where you can do it, we build the material so it is testable and revise on what you find; where you cannot, we say plainly that the scores are a proxy rather than an answer."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Patient education content","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/patient-education-content/"}]}];

